003353}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6209198669944564},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-121","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"121","title":"Proposing an amendment to the Constitution of the United States relating to contributions and expenditures intended to affect elections.","introducedDate":"2025-09-11","latestActionDate":"2025-09-11","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001205","cosponsorCount":46,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/121","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Campaign finance regulation directly affects lobbying firms and election-law practitioners who advise on contribution and expenditure compliance."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.8,"rationale":"Advocacy organizations that spend money on election-related communications would face new regulatory constraints on their expenditures."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Limits on campaign spending could affect demand for political advertising across broadcast, digital, and print media platforms."},{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.55,"rationale":"Financial institutions that make political expenditures or operate PACs could face new restrictions on corporate election spending."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution proposes a constitutional amendment that would allow Congress and state governments to set limits on how much money candidates and others can raise and spend to influence elections. It would also permit lawmakers to treat corporations and other legal entities differently from individual people, including by banning corporate spending on elections. The amendment would require ratification by the states to take effect.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who deals with a federal agency.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"F000454","score":0.04685049700852134},"overlapCount":46},"meaning":{"percentile":56.5629442426157,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6209198669944564}},{"billId":"119-hr-51","bioguideId":"C001055","cycle":2026,"score":0.007715609322341119,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":9750,"donorShare":0.01313490634636643,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.0035822471853726627},{"industry":"government","donorAmount":4000,"donorShare":0.005388679526714433,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.002204459806383177},{"industry":"real-estate","donorAmount":4500,"donorShare":0.0060622644675537365,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.0019289023305852793}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6209198669944564},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-51","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"51","title":"Washington, D.C. Admission Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Oversight and Government Reform, and in addition to the Committees on Rules, Armed Services, the Judiciary, and Energy and Commerce, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":["Congressional districts and representation","Congressional elections","Constitution and constitutional amendments","District of Columbia","Elections, voting, political campaign regulation","Government buildings, facilities, and property","House of Representatives","Legislative rules and procedure","Members of Congress","Presidents and presidential powers, Vice Presidents","State and local government operations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"N000147","cosponsorCount":204,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/51","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill fundamentally restructures the governance of the District, creating a new state government and redefining the federal district."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Statehood would change property tax authority and land-use regulation for most of the current District, affecting developers and property owners."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The transition of DC courts, judicial proceedings, and contractual obligations to state authority would affect legal practice and litigation in the new jurisdiction."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would admit most of the current District of Columbia as the 51st state, called Washington, Douglass Commonwealth, with two senators and one representative in Congress. A small federal district containing the Capitol, White House, Supreme Court, and nearby monuments would remain as the seat of the federal government. The bill sets up a transition process for DC laws, courts, contracts, and obligations to continue under state authority, and creates a commission to manage the shift to statehood.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who votes.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001245","score":0.050448841212731466},"overlapCount":197},"meaning":{"percentile":37.40325383035855,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.620919866